# Code-Layout, Readability and Reusability

## 1. Introduction

The project follows an organized development approach to make the ServiceNow configuration and ACL scripts easy to understand, maintain, and reuse.

## 2. Development Structure

The project is organized into separate components for:

* User creation
* Role creation
* Table creation
* Table fields
* Record creation
* READ ACL
* CREATE ACL
* WRITE ACL
* DELETE ACL

This separation makes the project easier to manage and test.

## 3. Organized Access Control

Each CRUD operation is controlled through its respective ACL.

This provides a clear structure for managing permissions and identifying the purpose of each access-control rule.

## 4. Readability

The ACL scripts are written using clear conditions and meaningful comments.

The project avoids unnecessary complexity so that team members can understand the access-control logic during development and testing.

## 5. Reusability

The ACL-based approach can be reused for other ServiceNow tables and similar access-control requirements.

The same structure can be extended by modifying:

* Roles
* Table names
* Field conditions
* Access requirements
* ACL scripts

## 6. Maintainability

The organized ACL structure makes it easier to:

* Identify individual permissions.
* Modify access conditions.
* Test specific operations.
* Add new roles.
* Add new access requirements.
* Troubleshoot permission-related issues.

## 7. Conclusion

A structured code and configuration layout improves readability, reusability, and maintainability of the Script-Controlled ACL solution.
